course: dictionary
lesson: 5723
title: Loosely: not tight, not exact, not firm
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Loosely is an adverb. It tells you how something is done, and it has three main meanings: not tightly, not exactly, and not firmly joined or organised. The words around it show you which meaning fits. «Tie the rope loosely» is about how tight a knot is, so loosely means not tightly.
explain: «The film is loosely based on a true story.» The words based on a true story are your clue. Loosely here means not exactly, in a general way: the film is inspired by real events, but it may change details.
explain: Use the swap check. Put your meaning into the sentence in place of the word. «Tie the rope not tightly» still makes sense, so that meaning is right. «The film is not exactly based on a true story» also makes sense, so that meaning fits too.
fact: Loosely is an adverb, and its comparative form is more loosely.
fact: «Loosely» can mean not tightly: «Tie the rope loosely».
fact: «Loosely» can mean not exactly or strictly: «The film is loosely based on a true story».
fact: «Loosely» can mean not firmly joined or organised: «a loosely connected group».
fact: In first aid, «Burns are cooled under cool running water for 20 minutes, then covered loosely with cling film or a clean dressing.»
exercise: «Tie the rope loosely.» What does loosely mean here? | Very fast | Very far away | Very tightly | *Not tightly
exercise: «The film is loosely based on a true story.» What does loosely tell you? | *Not exactly true, only in a general way | The film was made very quickly | The film was filmed outdoors | The story is exactly true
exercise: In «a loosely connected group», what does loosely mean? | *Not firmly joined or organised | Very strongly joined | Very large | Very quiet
test: In «Burns are cooled under cool running water for 20 minutes, then covered loosely», what does loosely mean? | *Not tightly | Very tightly | Under water | For 20 minutes
test: What does loosely mean in «The jaw bones are loosely joined to the skull»? | Very firmly | Joined by a rope | Joined very quickly | *Not firmly joined
exam: Which is the best definition of loosely? | Very tightly and very exactly | *Not tightly, not exactly, or not firmly joined | Very firmly and always in order | Very quickly and very loudly
